    "description": "<blockquote>\n<p>The Federated Knowledge Graph Ecosystem enables interoperable, distributed access to research knowledge by connecting RDF triplestores, community-curated knowledge bases, and shared semantic standards. However, many research outputs of high practical relevance&mdash;such as software, analytical workflows, images, or 3D data&mdash;remain difficult to integrate into such ecosystems, as they are typically distributed as binary artefacts outside the RDF world.</p>\n<p>FAIR Digital Objects (FDOs) address this gap by encapsulating digital artefacts together with metadata and identifiers in a machine-actionable form, but concrete, executable reference implementations remain scarce. This paper presents a minimal, standards-based method and software reference implementation that operationalises core FDO concepts using lightweight metadata packaging and semantic crosswalks. Starting from a self-contained ZIP archive containing binary content together with&nbsp;<em>CITATION.cff </em>and <em>MD.cff</em> metadata, the approach generates an RDF representation that exposes object structure, file-level distributions, and provenance information.</p>\n<p>The resulting RDF output can be ingested into triplestores and queried via SPARQL, enabling binary research artefacts to participate as first-class entities in Federated Knowledge Graph Ecosystems. The implementation is intentionally scoped as a pragmatic entry point, demonstrating how FAIR Digital Objects can be made actionable using existing standards and tooling.</p>\n</blockquote>",
